WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.438
Picture a family group chat.

2
00:00:04.438 --> 00:00:08.473
Your aunt posts a photo, and everyone reads it in their own time.

3
00:00:08.733 --> 00:00:11.293
Each phone remembers where its owner stopped.

4
00:00:11.293 --> 00:00:14.080
And your reading deletes nothing for anyone else.

5
00:00:14.333 --> 00:00:18.896
Now a payments company wants a new program to study last week's payments.

6
00:00:18.896 --> 00:00:22.272
With the broker from the last lesson, it gets nothing.

7
00:00:22.272 --> 00:00:22.522
Why?

8
00:00:22.522 --> 00:00:24.835
And what would a chat do differently?

9
00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:27.925
Here's the broker's queue, with four payments.

10
00:00:27.925 --> 00:00:31.549
The ledger reads one, says done, and the broker deletes it.

11
00:00:31.549 --> 00:00:33.760
A message is used up once it's read.

12
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:36.932
Letters work that way, and so do to-do lists.

13
00:00:36.932 --> 00:00:42.143
It's a library that shreds each book as soon as the first reader brings it back.

14
00:00:42.143 --> 00:00:43.316
Very tidy shelves.

15
00:00:43.316 --> 00:00:45.205
A very unhappy second reader.

16
00:00:45.467 --> 00:00:46.769
It costs three things.

17
00:00:46.769 --> 00:00:52.095
To feed four programs, the broker keeps four queues, with a copy of every payment in each.

18
00:00:52.095 --> 00:00:54.108
A new program starts with nothing.

19
00:00:54.108 --> 00:01:00.085
And if a bug means yesterday's payments must be worked through again, the queue can't give them back.

20
00:01:00.333 --> 00:01:05.515
Wouldn't it be nice if every payment stayed, and each reader kept only its own place?

21
00:01:05.515 --> 00:01:06.491
That's the chat.

22
00:01:06.767 --> 00:01:08.309
So, keep every payment.

23
00:01:08.309 --> 00:01:13.137
The payments program adds each one to the end of a file that only grows.

24
00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:18.159
Every payment gets a number, its place in the file: 0, 1, 2, 3.

25
00:01:18.159 --> 00:01:20.048
The number never changes.

26
00:01:20.333 --> 00:01:24.967
Each reader remembers one thing: the number of the next payment it wants.

27
00:01:24.967 --> 00:01:27.824
The ledger has read everything, so it's at 4.

28
00:01:27.824 --> 00:01:29.475
The fraud check is slower.

29
00:01:29.475 --> 00:01:30.109
It's at 2.

30
00:01:30.367 --> 00:01:31.158
Map it out.

31
00:01:31.158 --> 00:01:32.669
The chat is the file.

32
00:01:32.669 --> 00:01:34.324
Each post is a payment.

33
00:01:34.324 --> 00:01:38.137
And each phone's place is one number for each reader.

34
00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:41.083
Now the fraud check reads two payments.

35
00:01:41.083 --> 00:01:42.802
What changes in the file?

36
00:01:42.802 --> 00:01:43.903
Pause and guess.

37
00:01:47.133 --> 00:01:47.643
Nothing.

38
00:01:47.643 --> 00:01:50.513
Its number moves from 2 to 4, and that's all.

39
00:01:50.513 --> 00:01:53.319
The records are still there for anyone else.

40
00:01:53.567 --> 00:01:55.328
A new payment arrives.

41
00:01:55.328 --> 00:01:58.049
Account 42 gets a refund of 12.50.

42
00:01:58.049 --> 00:02:00.611
It goes at the end, as number 4.

43
00:02:00.611 --> 00:02:01.892
No reader moves.

44
00:02:01.892 --> 00:02:04.373
Both now have one more to read.

45
00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:07.615
The ledger reads it, and moves to 5, the end.

46
00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:10.587
So, is a payment used up once it's read?

47
00:02:10.587 --> 00:02:15.558
The new program, the weekly report, starts at 0, the first payment of all.

48
00:02:15.558 --> 00:02:16.901
Nobody else noticed.

49
00:02:17.133 --> 00:02:19.298
And the bug in yesterday's sums?

50
00:02:19.298 --> 00:02:22.952
Fix it, move the ledger's number back, and read again.

51
00:02:22.952 --> 00:02:24.102
One number moved.

52
00:02:24.102 --> 00:02:25.117
Nothing copied.

53
00:02:25.467 --> 00:02:27.950
The chat picture breaks in two places.

54
00:02:27.950 --> 00:02:30.826
A chat may hide old posts from a new member.

55
00:02:30.826 --> 00:02:32.591
A log shows them to anyone.

56
00:02:32.833 --> 00:02:38.535
And a chat lets you edit or delete a post, while a log never changes a record once it's written.

57
00:02:38.535 --> 00:02:41.743
To correct something, you add a new record at the end.

58
00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:46.724
This is called a log: an ordered list of records that only grows at the end.

59
00:02:46.724 --> 00:02:50.888
Each reader's number is its position, the next record it will read.

60
00:02:51.167 --> 00:02:56.658
Kafka is a program that keeps logs like this on disk, and lets many programs read them.

61
00:02:56.658 --> 00:02:58.867
In Kafka, each payment is a record.

62
00:02:58.867 --> 00:03:03.411
The program that adds them is a producer, and the readers are consumers.

63
00:03:03.633 --> 00:03:06.314
And the log's name, payments, is a topic.

64
00:03:06.314 --> 00:03:10.303
Here a topic is one log; the next lesson splits it into many.

65
00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:11.521
Two cautions.

66
00:03:11.521 --> 00:03:14.212
If reading deletes nothing, what does?

67
00:03:14.212 --> 00:03:19.028
Only a limit you set on each topic: how old, or how big, it may get.

68
00:03:19.028 --> 00:03:21.507
By default, Kafka keeps seven days.

69
00:03:21.733 --> 00:03:31.660
And since every record stays, readable by any program allowed to read the topic, never write anything you aren't allowed to keep for days, such as full card numbers.

70
00:03:31.933 --> 00:03:36.574
Leave a busy chat unread for a day, and the app still knows where you stopped.

71
00:03:36.574 --> 00:03:38.240
It saved one number for you.

72
00:03:38.533 --> 00:03:42.891
So: one file you only add to, and a bookmark for each reader.

73
00:03:42.891 --> 00:03:44.677
Reading moves a bookmark.

74
00:03:44.677 --> 00:03:45.892
It never deletes.

75
00:03:46.367 --> 00:03:47.376
One quick check.

76
00:03:47.376 --> 00:03:49.835
The weekly report reads three payments.

77
00:03:49.835 --> 00:03:53.555
Where is its number now, and what happened to the ledger's?

78
00:03:53.555 --> 00:03:54.564
Pause and think.

79
00:03:57.800 --> 00:03:59.251
The report is at 3.

80
00:03:59.251 --> 00:04:01.085
The ledger hasn't moved.

81
00:04:01.085 --> 00:04:02.307
It's still at 5.

82
00:04:02.307 --> 00:04:04.675
Readers never touch each other.
